## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to leverage Vertex AI Feature Store for end-to-end data governance while implementing practical rollback mechanisms in a beginner-friendly context. It evaluates understanding of how to wire together storage, training, deployment, and monitoring with clear guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Vertex AI Feature Store for centralized feature management
- Dataflow for batch feature updates and governance
- BigQuery for drift monitoring and lineage tracking
- Canary deployments with automated rollback
- Data Catalog for metadata and provenance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal illustrative snippet (pseudo-code)
import {FeatureStoreServiceClient} from '@google-cloud/aiplatform';

const client = new FeatureStoreServiceClient({project, location});

async function getFeatureValues(entityId) {
  const [entity] = await client.readFeatureValues({
    featurestore: FEATURESTORE_NAME,
    entityType: 'user',
    entityId: entityId,
  });
  return entity.featureValues;
}

async function checkDriftAndRollback(currentModelId, previousModelId) {
  const driftScore = await queryBigQueryDriftMetrics();
  if (driftScore > DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
    await switchEndpointTraffic(previousModelId);
    console.log('Rolled back due to drift:', driftScore);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a scalable, compliant, and observable MLOps workflow that supports multi-tenant isolation while meeting real-time processing requirements and governance standards.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant architecture with per-tenant resource isolation
- Real-time stream processing with PII redaction at ingestion
- Feature store management for tenant-specific model training
- Canary deployment strategies for safe model rollouts
- Model explainability and drift detection with automated rollback
- Comprehensive data lineage and governance tracking
- Cost management controls at the tenant level
- Late-arrival data handling within specified time windows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: canary deployment decision logic
if (tenantDrift[tenant] < DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
  promoteCanaryToProduction(tenant);
} else {
  rollbackToStableModel(tenant);
  triggerAlert(tenant, 'Model drift detected');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution across tenants?
- What strategies would you use for optimizing costs in a multi-tenant environment?
- How would you ensure data consistency when processing late-arriving events?

